    {
      "id": 1,
      "text": "Kerens was incorporated in 1881, marking its formal start as a town in Navarro County.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        6
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://ci.kerens.tx.us/community/history/",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The town was named after Judge Richard C., a railroad official who helped survey the area for the narrow‑gauge line.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kerens%2C_Texas",
      "category": "people",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "By the mid‑1890s Kerens boasted three cotton gin‑mills, four grocery stores, two hotels, two drug stores, a wagonmaker, and the weekly Navarro Blade newspaper.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.tshaonline.org/handbook/entries/kerens-tx",
      "category": "economy",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Kerens is the birthplace of Big Tex, the giant cowboy that first appeared at the State Fair of Texas.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        23
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kerens%2C_Texas",
      "category": "people",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "In 1949 residents erected a 49‑foot‑tall Santa Claus from iron drill casing and papier‑mâché to boost holiday sales.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        24
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kerens%2C_Texas",
      "category": "culture",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "Two years later, in 1951, the Santa’s parts were sold to State Fair president Thornton and transformed into a cowboy for the annual fair.",
